            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SizzleCakes"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good! Tastes just like the ahi that was served at a 4 star restaurant that I used to work. I served mine over rice."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Deanna"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I got some beautiful sashimi grade tuna in my sizzlefish box. I marinated in spicy sesame oil, sesame oil, wasabi powder, touch of water and liquid aminos. Put on field greens with salt and pepper. OMG! Thanks for the recipe suggestion!",
            "datePublished": "2020-08-02T00:42:37.227Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Dana Levy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have made this several times and it never disappoints. However, as is to me it's a little bland. I add about 1 tablespoon of black sesame seeds, 1 teaspoon of sesame oil and 1 teaspoon of soy sauce along with 1 tablespoon of lime juice. I saw the comment about the wasabi fumi furikake. I found it at a local Asian market and I live in a small town. It's a very good base to make something your own.",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-06T03:09:21.250Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CookinginColorado"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent. I could not find the wasabi fumi furikake (at Whole Foods), so I used a blend they offer of sea salt, garlic and sesame seeds as a substitute. Made for a milder taste, I suspect, but, for me, the best recipe is one you can vary according to what you have :-). I served it over greens, and it was delicious. My husband *loved* it.",
            "datePublished": "2011-07-29T18:21:25.587Z"
          }
        ],
